 1 big thing: A Nobel for neutrinos 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Illustration: Aïda Amer/Axios Belgian-born scientist Francis Halzen won the Nobel Prize in physics today for his efforts to demystify a rare group of neutrinos — tiny cosmic particles that could hint at how the universe evolved.

 Halzen told the Nobel Physics Committee: "It was a great surprise, and I obviously didn't expect it." 🔎 Neutrinos have no electric charge and extraordinarily small mass, making them hard to detect. 

 Yet they are everywhere. They spew from stars like the sun, and trillions zip through our bodies every second. 👩‍🔬 Scientists can't glimpse them zooming around on their own, so they measure what happens when they collide with other bits of matter. 

 But neutrinos bump into other particles only rarely, so researchers build large detectors to improve their odds of catching collisions. 🧊 Halzen had the idea to turn the vast Antarctic ice into such a detector. 

 The Nobel committee said his work was instrumental to the construction of the IceCube Neutrino Observatory in Antarctica, which glimpsed the first high-energy neutrinos from faraway galaxies. Go deeper ... Read the announcement . 

 
 
